,"committee":"Planning Policy and Local Plan Committee","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://tdcdemocracy.tendringdc.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-01","committee":"Council","time":"7.30 pm","status":"Confirmed; Extraordinary Meeting","webcast":true,"url":"https://tdcdemocracy.tendringdc.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-15","committee":"Resources and Services Overview and Scrutiny Committee","time":"7.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://tdcdemocracy.tendringdc.gov.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"\u00a31.000m allocation for unspecified general capacity lacks explanation","lead":"Unexplained capacity allocations may signal staffing, IT, or operational constraints that could affect service delivery and project execution.","iso":"2026-07-24","paper_title":"A1 Report - Financial Outturn 2025-26","paper_url":"https://tdcdemocracy.tendringdc.gov.uk/documents/s82609/82609.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Financial planning explicitly framed within interim period before April 2028 unitary authority transition","lead":"Major restructuring creates execution risk; service continuity and project delivery could be disrupted during transition, and the council's ability to maintain financial stability while managing reorganisation is uncertain.","iso":"2026-07-24","paper_title":"A1 Report - Financial Outturn 2025-26","paper_url":"https://tdcdemocracy.tendringdc.gov.uk/documents/s82609/82609.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"New social housing regulation creating unquantified future costs for Housing Revenue Account","lead":"New regulatory requirements could require significant HRA spending, and reserves of \u00a33.996m provide limited buffer for unexpected compliance costs.","iso":"2026-07-24","paper_title":"A1 Report - Financial Outturn 2025-26","paper_url":"https://tdcdemocracy.tendringdc.gov.uk/documents/s82609/82609.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-07-24","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"A1 Report - Financial Outturn 2025-26","paper_url":"https://tdcdemocracy.tendringdc.gov.uk/documents/s82609/82609.pdf","headline":"\u00a31.000m allocation for unspecified general capacity lacks explanation","detail":"The council is allocating \u00a31.000m to support general capacity within the council, to be spent at the Chief Executive's discretion. No explanation is provided for what capacity constraints exist or why this funding is required at this point.","category":"other","matters":"Unexplained capacity allocations may signal staffing, IT, or operational constraints that could affect service delivery and project execution.","figures":"\u00a31.000m allocation for general capacity in 2026/27","quote":"in respect of the additional \u00a31.000m set aside to support general capacity within the Council, authorises the Chief Executive, in consultation with the Portfolio Holder for Corporate Finance and Governance, to utilise this funding during the year","material":true},{"iso":"2026-07-24","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"A1 Report - Financial Outturn 2025-26","paper_url":"https://tdcdemocracy.tendringdc.gov.uk/documents/s82609/82609.pdf","headline":"Financial planning explicitly framed within interim period before April 2028 unitary authority transition","detail":"The council's two-year financial plan is structured around the 1 April 2028 vesting date for the new unitary authority, described as a significant change in the financial landscape. Budget decisions explicitly assess deliverability before March 2028, and the council notes that the outturn position forms an important element of planning for this major organisational change.","category":"governance","matters":"Major restructuring creates execution risk; service continuity and project delivery could be disrupted during transition, and the council's ability to maintain financial stability while managing reorganisation is uncertain.","figures":"Unitary authority vesting date: 1 April 2028; two-year financial plan extends to this date","quote":"the Council's financial position is now being considered within an interim period before new / successor Unitary Councils are established from the current expected date of 1 April 2028","material":true},{"iso":"2026-07-24","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"A1 Report - Financial Outturn 2025-26","paper_url":"https://tdcdemocracy.tendringdc.gov.uk/documents/s82609/82609.pdf","headline":"New social housing regulation creating unquantified future costs for Housing Revenue Account","detail":"The council notes it remains alert to changes required in managing its housing stock due to the new era of social housing regulation. These impacts will need to be reflected in the 30-year HRA Business Plan, but no specifics or cost projections are provided. The HRA general balance has declined from \u00a34.052m to \u00a33.996m.","category":"demand","matters":"New regulatory requirements could require significant HRA spending, and reserves of \u00a33.996m provide limited buffer for unexpected compliance costs.","figures":"HRA general balance \u00a33.996m (down from \u00a34.052m); HRA favourable variance \u00a31.652m transferred to balances","quote":"Council remains alert to any changes that may be required in managing its housing stock, including the on-going impact from the new era of social housing regulation, which will need to be reflected in the 30 Year Business Plan as necessary","material":true},{"iso":"2026-07-24","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"A1 Report - Financial Outturn 2025-26","paper_url":"https://tdcdemocracy.tendringdc.gov.uk/documents/s82609/82609.pdf","headline":"\u00a319.778m in revenue carry-forwards (85% of total variance) remain unsettled and subject to post-outturn review","detail":"The council's \u00a323.167m total variance comprises \u00a319.778m in requested carry-forwards and \u00a33.389m final favourable variance. Carry-forwards remain subject to separate review to verify projects support corporate priorities and are deliverable before 31 March 2028. Amounts not subsequently agreed will transfer to corporate investment funds. This high proportion of unresolved spend at year-end is unusual and suggests potential delivery challenges.","category":"savings","matters":"Unsettled spend of this magnitude indicates either budget planning issues or execution delays, creating uncertainty about whether planned programmes are realistic and will deliver.","figures":"\u00a319.778m carry-forwards of \u00a323.167m total variance; \u00a33.389m final favourable variance","quote":"requested revenue carryforwards total \u00a319.778m which remain subject to on-going and separate review. Once the review has been completed and agreed under an existing delegation to the Portfolio Holder for Finance and Governance, the money relating to any items not agreed will be transferred to the relevant Corporate Investment Fund","material":true},{"iso":"2026-07-24","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"A2 Report - Treasury Outturn 2025-26","paper_url":"https://tdcdemocracy.tendringdc.gov.uk/documents/s82606/82606.pdf","headline":"Bank of England's inflation forecast has reversed sharply, now expecting CPI could breach 4.5% against earlier 2% target forecast","detail":"Bank of England's current inflation outlook has shifted significantly. Where they previously expected inflation to reach their 2% target by 2026-27, forecasts now suggest CPI could breach 4.5% in 2026. This represents substantial economic uncertainty that affects council service cost projections and tax base assumptions.","category":"other","matters":"Elevated inflation creates material planning risk for multi-year budgets; service costs and demand pressures will be harder to forecast; council's treasury assumptions about future interest rates and economic environment may require revision","figures":"CPI forecast potentially 4.5% in 2026, versus earlier forecast of 2% target by 2026-27","quote":"Responding to inflation continues to be a challenge for the Bank of England with their current forecast suggesting CPI could breach 4.5% in 2026, which is a stark contrast to their earlier forecast which suggested inflation would reach their target amount of 2% from 2026/27.","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-07-24"},"name":"Tendring DC","findings_total":20,"news_drafts_total":3}}